GDBM  - The GNU database manager.  Includes [1mdbm [22mand [1mndbm [22mcompata-
bility. (Version 1.8.3.)


GNU dbm is a library of routines that  manages  data  files  that
contain  key/data pairs.  The access provided is that of storing,
retrieval, and deletion by key and a non-sorted traversal of  all
keys.   A  process  is  allowed to use multiple data files at the
same time.

A process that opens a gdbm file is designated as a "reader" or a
"writer".   Only one writer may open a gdbm file and many readers
may open the file.  Readers and writers can  not  open  the  gdbm
file at the same time. The procedure for opening a gdbm file is:

  GDBM_FILE dbf;

  dbf  =  gdbm_open  (  name,  block_size,  read_write, mode, fa-
tal_func )

[4mName[24m is the name of the file (the complete name,  gdbm  does  not
append any characters to this name).  [4mBlock_size[24m is the size of a
single transfer from disk to memory. This  parameter  is  ignored
unless  the  file is a new file.  The minimum size is 512.  If it
is less than 512, dbm will use the stat block size for  the  file
system.  [4mRead_write[24m can have one of the following values:
reader
writer
writer - if database does not exist create new one
writer - create new database regardless if one exists
For the last three (writers of the database) the following may be
added added  to  [4mread_write[24m  by  bitwise  or:  which  causes  all
database  operations  to  be  synchronized to the disk, and which
prevents the library from performing any locking on the  database
file.  The option is now obsolete, since gdbm defaults to no-sync
mode.
[4mMode[24m is the file mode (see [1mchmod(2) [22mand [1mopen(2)[22m) if the  file  is
created. [4m(*Fatal_func)[24m [4m()[24m is a function for dbm to call if it de-
tects a fatal error. The only parameter of  this  function  is  a
string.   If  the value of 0 is provided, gdbm will use a default
function.

The return value [4mdbf[24m is the pointer needed by all other  routines
to  access  that  gdbm  file.  If the return is the NULL pointer,
[1mgdbm_open [22mwas  not  successful.   The  errors  can  be  found  in
[4mgdbm_errno[24m  for gdbm errors and in [4merrno[24m for system errors.  (For
error codes, see gdbmerrno.h.)

In all of the following calls, the parameter [4mdbf[24m  refers  to  the
pointer returned from [1mgdbm_open[22m.

It  is  important that every file opened is also closed.  This is
needed to update the reader/writer count on the  file.   This  is
done by:

  gdbm_close (dbf);


The database is used by 3 primary routines.  The first stores da-
ta in the database.

  ret = gdbm_store ( dbf, key, content, flag )

[4mDbf[24m is the pointer returned by [1mgdbm_open[22m.  [4mKey[24m is the  key  data.
[4mContent[24m is the data to be associated with the [4mkey[24m.  [4mFlag[24m can have
one of the following values:
insert only, generate an error if key exists
replace contents if key exists.

If a reader calls [1mgdbm_store[22m, the return value will be   -1.   If
called  with  GDBM_INSERT  and [4mkey[24m is in the database, the return
value will be 1.  Otherwise, the return value is 0.

