To search for some data:

  content = gdbm_fetch ( dbf, key )

[4mDbf[24m is the pointer returned by [1mgdbm_open[22m.  [4mKey[24m is the key data.


If the [4mdptr[24m element of the return value  is  NULL,  no  data  was
found.   Otherwise the return value is a pointer to the found da-
ta.  The storage space for the [4mdptr[24m element  is  allocated  using
[1mmalloc(3C)[22m.   [1mGdbm  [4m[22mdoes[24m [4mnot[24m [4mautomatically[24m [4mfree[24m [4mthis[24m [4mdata.[24m  [4mIt[24m [4mis[0m
[4mthe[24m [4mprogrammer’s[24m [4mresponsibility[24m [4mto[24m [4mfree[24m [4mthis[24m [4mstorage[24m [4mwhen[24m  [4mit[24m  [4mis[0m
[4mno[24m [4mlonger[24m [4mneeded.[0m


To search for some data, without retrieving it:

  ret = gdbm_exists ( dbf, key )

[4mDbf[24m is the pointer returned by [1mgdbm_open[22m.  [4mKey[24m is the key data to
search for.

If the [4mkey[24m is found within the database,  the  return  value  [4mret[0m
will be true.  If nothing appropiate is found, [4mret[24m will be false.
This routine is useful  for  checking  for  the  existance  of  a
record,   without   performing  the  memory  allocation  done  by
[1mgdbm_fetch[22m.


To remove some data from the database:

  ret = gdbm_delete ( dbf, key )

[4mDbf[24m is the pointer returned by [1mgdbm_open[22m.  [4mKey[24m is the key data.

The return value is -1 if the item is  not  present  or  the  re-
quester  is  a reader.  The return value is 0 if there was a suc-
cessful delete.


The next two routines  allow  for  accessing  all  items  in  the
database.   This  access is not key sequential, but it is guaran-
teed to visit every key in the database once.  (The order has  to
do with the hash values.)

  key = gdbm_firstkey ( dbf )

  nextkey = gdbm_nextkey ( dbf, key )

[4mDbf[24m is the pointer returned by [1mgdbm_open[22m. [4mKey[24m is the key data.

The return values are both of type [1mdatum[22m.  If the [4mdptr[24m element of
the return value is NULL, there is no  first  key  or  next  key.
Again notice that [4mdptr[24m points to data allocated by [1mmalloc(3C) [22mand
[1mgdbm [22mwill not free it for you.

These functions were intended to visit the database in  read-only
algorithms, for instance, to validate the database or similar op-
erations.

File ‘visiting’ is based on a ‘hash table’.   [4mgdbm_delete[24m  re-ar-
ranges the hash table to make sure that any collisions in the ta-
ble do not leave some item ‘un-findable’.  The original key order
is  NOT  guaranteed  to remain unchanged in ALL instances.  It is
possible that some key will not be visited if  a  loop  like  the
following is executed:

   key = gdbm_firstkey ( dbf );
   while ( key.dptr ) {
      nextkey = gdbm_nextkey ( dbf, key );
      if ( some condition ) {
         gdbm_delete ( dbf, key );
         free ( key.dptr );
      }
      key = nextkey;
   }


The following routine should be used very infrequently.

  ret = gdbm_reorganize ( dbf )

If  you  have had a lot of deletions and would like to shrink the
space used by the [1mgdbm [22mfile, this  routine  will  reorganize  the
database.  [1mGdbm [22mwill not shorten the length of a [1mgdbm [22mfile except
by using  this  reorganization.   (Deleted  file  space  will  be
reused.)


Unless  your  database  was  opened with the GDBM_SYNC flag, gdbm
does not wait for writes to be flushed to the disk before contin-
uing.   The  following  routine can be used to guarantee that the
database is physically written to the disk file.

  gdbm_sync ( dbf )

It will not return until the disk file state is syncronized  with
the in-memory state of the database.


To convert a [1mgdbm [22merror code into English text, use this routine:

  ret = gdbm_strerror ( errno )

Where [4merrno[24m is of type [4mgdbm_error[24m, usually  the  global  variable
[4mgdbm_errno[24m.  The appropiate phrase is returned.


[1mGdbm  [22mnow  supports  the ability to set certain options on an al-
ready open database.

  ret = gdbm_setopt ( dbf, option, value, size )

Where [4mdbf[24m is the return value from a previous call to  [1mgdbm_open[22m,
and  [4moption[24m specifies which option to set.  The valid options are
currently:

  [1mGDBM_CACHESIZE [22m- Set the size of the internal bucket
  cache. This option may only be set once on each [4mGDBM_FILE[0m
  descriptor, and is set automatically to 100 upon the first
  access to the database.

  [1mGDBM_FASTMODE [22m- Set [1mfast mode [22mto either on or off.  This
  allows [1mfast mode [22mto be toggled on an already open and
  active database. [4mvalue[24m (see below) should be set to either
  TRUE or FALSE.  [4mThis[24m [4moption[24m [4mis[24m [4mnow[24m [4mobsolete.[0m

  [1mGDBM_SYNCMODE [22m- Turn on or off file system synchronization  op-
erations.
  This  setting  defaults to off; [4mvalue[24m (see below) should be set
to either
  TRUE or FALSE.

  [1mGDBM_CENTFREE [22m- Set [1mcentral free block pool  [22mto  either  on  or
off.
  The default is off, which is how previous versions of [1mGdbm[0m
  handled  free blocks. If set, this option causes all subsequent
free
  blocks to be placed in the [1mglobal [22mpool, allowing (in thoery)
  more file space to be reused more quickly.  [4mvalue[24m  (see  below)
should
  be set to either TRUE or FALSE.
  [4mNOTICE:[24m [4mThis[24m [4mfeature[24m [4mis[24m [4mstill[24m [4munder[24m [4mstudy.[0m

  [1mGDBM_COALESCEBLKS [22m- Set [1mfree block merging [22mto either on or off.
  The default is off, which is how previous versions of [1mGdbm[0m
  handled free blocks. If set, this option causes  adjacent  free
blocks
  to  be  merged.  This  can  become a CPU expensive process with
time, though,
  especially if used in conjunction with [1mGDBM_CENTFREE[22m. [4mvalue[0m
  (see below) should be set to either TRUE or FALSE.
  [4mNOTICE:[24m [4mThis[24m [4mfeature[24m [4mis[24m [4mstill[24m [4munder[24m [4mstudy.[0m

[4mvalue[24m is the value to set [4moption[24m  to,  specified  as  an  integer
pointer.   [4msize[24m is the size of the data pointed to by [4mvalue[24m.  The
return value will be -1 upon failure, or  0  upon  success.   The
global variable [4mgdbm_errno[24m will be set upon failure.

For instance, to set a database to use a cache of 10, after open-
ing it with [1mgdbm_open[22m, but prior to accessing it in any way,  the
following code could be used:

  int value = 10;

  ret = gdbm_setopt( dbf, GDBM_CACHESIZE, &value, sizeof(int));


If  the  database  was opened with the [1mGDBM_NOLOCK [22mflag, the user
may wish to perform their own file locking on the  database  file
in  order  to prevent multiple writers operating on the same file
simultaneously.

In order to support this, the [4mgdbm_fdesc[24m routine is provided.

  ret = gdbm_fdesc ( dbf )

Where [4mdbf[24m is the return value from a previous call to  [1mgdbm_open[22m.
The return value will be the file descriptor of the database.

The following two external variables may be useful:

[4mgdbm_errno[24m  is  the variable that contains more information about
gdbm errors.  (gdbm.h has the definitions of the error values and
defines gdbm_errno as an external variable.)
[4mgdbm_version[24m is the string containing the version information.


There  are  a few more things of interest.  First, [1mgdbm [22mfiles are
not "sparse".  You can copy them with the UNIX [1mcp(1) [22mcommand  and
they  will  not  expand in the copying process.  Also, there is a
compatibility mode for use with programs that  already  use  UNIX
[1mdbm[22m.   In  this  compatibility  mode, no gdbm file pointer is re-
quired by the programmer, and only one file may be  opened  at  a
time.  All users in compatibility mode are assumed to be writers.
If the [1mgdbm [22mfile is a read only, it will fail as  a  writer,  but
will  also  try to open it as a reader.  All returned pointers in
datum structures point to data that [1mgdbm [22mWILL free.  They  should
be treated as static pointers (as standard UNIX [1mdbm [22mdoes).


This library is accessed by specifying [4m-lgdbm[24m as the last parame-
ter to the compile line, e.g.:

        gcc -o prog prog.c -lgdbm

If you wish to use the [1mdbm [22mor [1mndbm  [22mcompatibility  routines,  you
must link in the [4mgdbm_compat[24m library as well.  For example:

        gcc -o prog proc.c -lgdbm -lgdbm_compat


